From bf6ed39a4e3273891281210a0724210c23fd1e36 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Sriram Nambakam <snambakam@linux.microsoft.com>
Date: Wed, 29 Jul 2026 00:18:05 -0700
Subject: [RFC PATCH v1 000/132] VBS/VSM-on-KVM: KVM VM planes and
 the in-kernel secure VM plane (VTL)
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

This RFC implements a Virtualization-Based Security (VBS) / Hyper-V VSM style
secure environment on top of KVM VM planes. A higher-privilege "secure plane"
(analogous to a Hyper-V VTL) runs alongside the normal plane, with its own EPT
root and memory that the normal plane cannot touch; the two communicate through
VTL call/return hypercalls rather than a trampoline.

This series is the *full* plane stack linearized as a clean, bisectable history
directly on mainline (v7.2-rc5). It folds together:

  - Joerg Roedel's KVM VM planes work (the in-kernel plane infrastructure);
  - Paolo Bonzini's original VM-plane model that Joerg's tree carries forward;
    and
  - the safe-tee VBS/VSM secure-plane patches layered on top.

Authorship is preserved on every commit; the shortlog below is grouped by
author. The result applies directly on upstream master with no external base to
hunt down.

On top of the plane infrastructure the VBS/VSM portion adds:

  - in-kernel plane switching and VBS VTL call/return + cross-plane
    set-mem-attrs hypercalls (arch-neutral helper + x86 wiring);
  - a secure-plane monitor backend and park/secure_monitor loop;
  - rootfs_initcall plane setup that loads the secure-plane ELF payload and
    runs the HEKI seal;
  - normal-plane access denial to secure-plane memory; and
  - assorted plane/selftest fixes (realmode trampoline skip, plane-fd
    KVM_CHECK_EXTENSION, split-IRQ-chip selftests).

Repositories (safe-tee GitHub org)
==================================

Everything needed to fetch, apply, build, and test this series is published
under the safe-tee organization on GitHub:

  - kernel: https://github.com/safe-tee/linux
      branch kvm-planes-v7.2-rc5     this series: the full VM-planes + VBS/VSM
                                     stack linearized on mainline v7.2-rc5
                                     (the base-commit listed below).
      branch vm-planes-merged        the original merge-based integration tree
                                     this series was linearized from.

  - qemu:   https://github.com/safe-tee/qemu
      branch qemu-planes-v11.1.0-rc1 the QEMU counterpart series, linearized
                                     on mainline v11.1.0-rc1.

  - lvbs:   https://github.com/safe-tee/lvbs
      the build/test workspace, scripts, and documentation that tie the
      three trees together.

Build and test
==============

The lvbs repository documents the full build-and-run flow (kernel, QEMU,
secure kernel, and Fedora VM image) with VM planes enabled:

  https://github.com/safe-tee/lvbs/blob/main/docs/how-to/build.md

Quick start, from the lvbs repo root, with the linux, qemu, and lvbs trees
checked out side by side under a common workspace root:

  make kernel          # plane 0 bzImage + plane 1 vmlinux
  make secure-kernel   # standalone minimal plane-1 kernel
  make qemu            # qemu-system-x86_64
  make image           # fedora-kvm.raw (uses the secure kernel)

Design and reference material (VM planes architecture, LVBS boot process,
VBS backends, plane-switch sequence, and the LVBS patch overview) lives under
lvbs/docs/reference/.

Acknowledgments
===============

This work stands on top of, and is indebted to, several prior efforts:

  - Joerg Roedel, whose KVM VM planes tree (kvm-planes-v7.1) this series
    is layered directly on top of. His planes implementation provides the
    in-kernel plane infrastructure that the VBS/VSM secure plane relies on.

  - Paolo Bonzini, whose "[RFC PATCH 00/29] KVM: VM planes" introduced the
    VM plane concept to KVM as a common in-kernel model for AMD VMPLs, Intel
    TDX partitions, Hyper-V VTLs, and ARM CCA planes. Joerg Roedel's tree
    (which this series builds on) carries that work forward.
    https://lwn.net/Articles/1016113/

  - The prior Linux VBS (LVBS) and Hypervisor-Enforced Kernel Integrity
    (HEKI) work by James Morris, Thara Gopinath, Wei Liu,
    Madhavan Venkataraman, and Mickaël Salaün, posted as
    "[RFC PATCH v2 00/19] Hypervisor-Enforced Kernel Integrity (HEKI)".
    The kernel-sealing model used here follows their design.

  - James Bottomley and James Morris, for their ongoing work on VSM-on-KVM,
    which informed the design and direction of this series.

Posted as an RFC to gather feedback on the VBS-on-KVM plane model and the
VTL hypercall ABI. Comments welcome.

